      "writtenOn": "2026-07-14T22:20:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This duplicates the `__builtin_mul_overflow` check that is now already performed inside `sys_alloc`. Furthermore, it introduces an inconsistency: if overflow occurs here, `plat_calloc` returns `NULL`, whereas `sys_alloc` calls `k_oops()`. Callers might not check for `NULL` returns since `sys_alloc` normally panics on error.\n\nYou can simplify this and ensure consistent behavior by just calling `sys_alloc` directly and using `sys_alloc_usable_size` to get the correct memset length:\n```c\nstatic inline void *plat_calloc(size_t count, size_t size)\n{\n\tvoid *ptr \u003d sys_alloc(count, size);\n\tif (ptr) {\n\t\tmemset(ptr, 0, sys_alloc_usable_size(ptr));\n\t}\n\treturn ptr;\n}\n```",
